    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nAs described, this is already the case as best as it can be. Neighborhood pattern is matched against two *possibly multibyte* characters: one to the left and one to the right of the cursor. This was [added about 10 months ago](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/commit/297fd93d4836f12f4ecca2d77b1a09b5b88a2115).\n\nThe case you describe indeed doesn't work neither with provided pattern (`[^%a].`) nor with default one (`[^%a].`). But it works with a bit more confusing looking `^[^%a\\\\]`: i.e. insert pair only if neighborhood *starts* not with letter or `\\`.\n\nNow, there are indeed some problems here:\n- The [documentation](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/c163117900c17d4abf30bc09452a261c8536060c/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L169) should mention \"multibyte characters\".\n- Default patterns might be more useful. All of them are in the form of \"if left character is not ...\", so the can be translated into `^[^...]` form to be more multibyte friendly. The slight problem here is that it doesn't showcase the idea of \"it matches against exactly two characters\".\n\nI'll address above two points. But as it stands, this issue already works as expected.",
